    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d103-a1">

                <pb facs="0248" n="192" xml:id="pg192"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosures.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Inman Steamship Company</hi>, (<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >limited</hi>,) <hi rendition="#small-caps">late Liverpool, New York
                                and Philadelphia Steamship Company</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Liverpool</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1875-03-08"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 8,
                        1875</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Sir</hi>: After a careful perusal of the
                    document you sent me on the subject of “fog-signals,” I beg to forward my
                    opinion at your request.</p>
                <p>In the United States of America the following signals, which I consider simple
                    and effective, are invariably used during fogs and in narrow channels, and I
                    think could with advantage be adopted in British waters, viz: <list>
                        <item>One short blast, “I am porting.”</item>
                        <item>Two short blasts, “I am starboarding.”</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>These are all that are used in the United States, but I would suggest another:
                    three short blasts, “I am stopped;” if a sailing ship, “I am in stays,” or
                    “sails aback.” These I consider sufficient; any more would make it
                    confusing.</p>
                <p>I remain, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JAMES KENNEDY.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">E. Inman</hi>, Esq.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
